WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.350
- You were speaking about
how you're recording

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:04.320
and all these beautiful
musicians y'all were getting

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.130
to get to make this music together.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.547
And you have a new album,

00:00:07.547 --> 00:00:09.330
"Great Songs from Stage and Screen."

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:10.610
I love your voice

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:13.280
because I, one of my favorite
singers is Rosemary Clooney.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.450
I love that whole style of singing

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:18.020
and I love that it's happy music.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.190
Was that on purpose?

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:21.060
To do something that was kind
of a little bit more happy

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.260
and not so melancholy?

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:23.120
- Yeah, well, you know,

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.000
we recorded most of this
prior to the pandemic

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.630
in London at Abbey road.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.040
So it's--
- Oh my God!

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.480
- It's fortunate that it is
upbeat and not, you know,

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.560
'cause we do do a lot of sad
albums as well, so luckily

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:36.800
the timing of this worked out,

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.260
'cause I don't know that,

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:39.690
you know, more depression would be

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.080
what people want right now but...

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.850
Yeah, as it turns out, you
know, it's very upbeat.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:46.940
It's every song is sort of a

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.740
pop version of a,

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.453
a song from a film or a Broadway show.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:57.760
And yeah, it's just supposed
to be fun and upbeat and,

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.000
you know, just it's a bubbly record.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.980
- I mean, it's a bubbly
record, but you're so gifted.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:04.160
And I was just curious,

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.120
did you start like young singing?

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.700
- You know what, I
started with Kelly Okey.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.860
(laughter)

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.339
- Well, that's why you're amazing Seth.

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:16.032
(laughter)

00:01:16.032 --> 00:01:18.050
- Yeah, you know, I, you know, I was,

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:21.210
my parents always felt that music was a,

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:25.610
an important part of the
developmental process of the brain.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:29.120
And, so they always
had music in the house.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.830
You know, my father played the guitar.

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.640
My parents signed me up for a

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.490
a church choir, when I was a kid.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:36.658
If you can believe that.

00:01:36.658 --> 00:01:39.270
- I was, and that's hilarious
now your style of writing,

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.910
but I was actually a
church choir kid as well.

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:43.570
- Yeah, I'm sure,

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.510
you were, it fit a little better.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:49.120
I was a little bit of a fish out of water.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.300
I was not, I was not a...

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.250
I probably shouldn't have been there.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.833
But, it was fun, I loved it it was great,

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.680
It was great fun and it was,

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.280
again, it was like it was really

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.410
helpful for developing
the voice and whatnot.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.250
And so that,

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:05.940
you know, made its way into

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.590
local shows, local musicals.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.773
My sister and I both really tried to stay,

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.330
you know, somewhere in that world,

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.160
even on just a local level.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.120
- Yeah, it's fun.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:16.953
Especially when you're a kid.

00:02:16.953 --> 00:02:18.400
I did all that stuff too.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.470
So is it true you trained as an adult

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:22.210
with the same teachers
who trained Sinatra?

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:24.890
Because you have a certain
flare that is Sinatra like,

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:25.900
that I love.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:26.733
- Well, they,

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:29.500
it was an elderly couple.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.520
They were at Lee and Sally Sweetland.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.593
They were both in their late nineties.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.770
And I trained with them
for at least 10 years.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:39.800
Probably longer than that.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.960
And they, yeah, I, you know

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.370
they had worked with Sinatra
at one point, I believe.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:47.150
I know they trained
Streisand at one point.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.296
I, I've never really done my homework

00:02:49.296 --> 00:02:53.293
and gotten the full rundown of what their,

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.570
what their celebrity roster is, but

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.750
I've gotten fragments
and it's very impressive.

